 1.1 An information-lossless $s$-state encoder consists of a finite state set $S$, an initial state, an update map $g:S\times\mathcal A\to S$, and an output map $f:S\times\mathcal A\to\{0,1\}^*$. It is information-lossless when for each initial state the map from finite input words to (concatenated output, final state) is injective. [F1, given, algebra]
 
-2.1 Let $L_E(x_0^{n-1})$ be its concatenated output length and set $\rho_s(x)=\inf_E\limsup_nL_E(x_0^{n-1})/n$, over information-lossless encoders with at most $s$ states. Define finite-state compressibility $\rho_{\rm FS}(x)=\inf_{s\ge1}\rho_s(x)$. The infima are nonincreasing in $s$ and bounded below by zero, so the limit in the equivalent notation $\lim_{s\to\infty}\rho_s(x)$ exists. [given, step 1.1, algebra] ∎
